{
  "name": "Making Room: When to Widen a Doorway for Wheelchair Access",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Deciding to widen a doorway for wheelchair access can feel like a big project, but it's often more straightforward than you might think, especially with the right approach. The key is to start with careful measurements. You need to look at the 'clear opening' at the narrowest point, which isn't always obvious. Then, consider the door's swing and how much maneuvering room you have on both sides. Sometimes, simply removing some trim, changing the hinges to offset ones, or reversing the door's swing can gain you those crucial extra inches without major demolition. For instance, a 30-inch door might only give you 28 inches of clear width. If your wheelchair needs 32 inches, you've got a gap to bridge. HH Remodeling LLC helps homeowners across the Denver area evaluate these situations daily. If widening means getting into structural changes or altering the frame, that's definitely when you'll want a professional to ensure everything is done correctly and safely, and to make sure it meets accessibility standards. Give us a call at 303-505-0866 if you're in the Denver, Aurora, or Colorado Springs area and need some expert advice.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
